        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Laser engraving wood: which laser to choose and how to start',
            'slug'    => 'laser-engraving-wood',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser engraving wood from scratch: diode or CO₂, which woods and plywood work, how to avoid burn marks and which xTool laser engraver for wood to choose.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'For laser engraving wood, a diode laser is the usual place to start: it engraves boards, plywood and wooden gifts in fine detail and cuts thin plywood. Choose a CO₂ laser when you want to cut thicker material faster, work with large sheets and produce in batches. Either way, the result depends on focus, air assist, the right material and a test on an offcut.' ],
                [ 'p', 'If you are looking for a laser engraver for wood, xTool offers four good starting points: the portable F2 with a 15 W diode laser, the enclosed S1 with a 20 or 40 W diode module, the M2, which pairs a diode laser with colour printing, and the 80 W P3 CO₂ laser. We explain how engraving differs from cutting, which wood to choose, how to avoid burn marks and which machine suits which project.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which laser for wood: diode or CO₂?' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Diode laser: engraving and thin plywood' ],
                [ 'p', 'A diode laser emits blue light at around 455 nm, which wood absorbs well – dark species even more readily than very pale ones. Its small spot reproduces lettering, logos and photos faithfully on boards, boxes and coasters. A diode cuts thin plywood, MDF, card and leather, and thicker material in several passes. It is smaller and cheaper than a CO₂ laser, which makes it the most common choice for home use and small workshops.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'CO₂ laser: faster cutting of thicker material' ],
                [ 'p', 'A CO₂ laser works in the infrared at 10.6 µm, a wavelength wood absorbs very well regardless of its colour. It usually has more power – in the xTool range, 80 W in the P3 versus 10–40 W in the diode modules – so it cuts thicker material faster, in fewer passes and generally with less charring on the edge. It is the machine for batches and large sheets, and it cuts clear acrylic too, but it costs more, takes up more space and needs efficient fume extraction.' ],
                [ 'p', 'IR, MOPA and UV lasers are tools for metal, plastics and glass, not wood – in the F2, wood is handled by the diode module.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Engraving vs cutting wood: what is the difference?' ],
                [ 'p', 'When engraving, the laser moves line by line and scorches a thin layer of wood. Darkness and depth depend on power, speed, line density and the number of passes – the same design can come out as a light brown mark or a deep, dark engraving. Photos are engraved as raster images with dithering, ideally on pale wood with an even grain.' ],
                [ 'p', 'When cutting, the laser follows a vector line until it burns through. The edge always darkens a little, and the shorter the beam dwells on one spot, the lighter it stays. That is why xTool recommends cutting thicker plywood at high power in several fast passes rather than one slow one.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The standard material for learning is 3 mm plywood – xTool sells birch and basswood plywood in this thickness for cutting and engraving on its machines. How thick a given machine cuts depends on power, wood species, glue, moisture and the number of passes, so test thicker boards on a sample before you buy a laser.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which woods work well with a laser and which cause problems?' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Good choices to start with' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Birch plywood: pale, even and stable, with high-contrast engraving. Choose plywood made for lasers – construction and exterior plywood often has thicker glue layers and voids where the cut won’t go through.',
                    'Basswood plywood: light and soft, easy to cut, good for models, decorations and prototypes.',
                    'Fine-grained hardwoods such as maple, beech, alder or cherry: even engraving on boards and boxes. Dark walnut gives less contrast.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h3', 'Materials that need care' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'MDF and glued boards: they cut and engrave evenly, but the binders – usually urea-formaldehyde resins – produce more smoke and odour than solid wood. xTool sells 3 mm MDF boards for laser work and recommends air assist with them. Don’t start without fume extraction.',
                    'Resinous and oily wood: in pine, spruce or larch, the soft earlywood burns deeper than the hard latewood, so engraving comes out uneven, and resin ignites more easily and soils the optics. Oily teak smokes more.',
                    'Treated, varnished and painted wood: preservatives, varnishes and paints release fumes of unknown composition when burned. Only process surfaces you know the make-up of, and avoid PVC veneers and edge banding – PVC releases hydrogen chloride.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h2', 'How to get clean engraving and neat cuts' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Focus: set the focal point on the surface of the material. Warped plywood blurs the engraving and may not cut through in places, so hold it flat with clamps or pins. The S1 and P3 measure the distance to the material automatically.',
                    'Air assist: it blows smoke and flame away from the cut, so edges are cleaner and the cut gets through more easily.',
                    'Masking tape: xTool recommends taping plywood on the design side for engraving and on both sides for cutting, without air bubbles. The tape reduces darkening of the surface and edges.',
                    'Support: a honeycomb bed lifts the material, smoke escapes underneath and fewer burn marks appear on the back.',
                    'Test grid: before a batch, burn squares at different power and speed on an offcut of the same board and save the best settings. xTool’s material settings library is a good starting point, but every batch of wood reacts a little differently.',
                    'Files: cutting needs vectors (SVG, DXF), while raster images (JPG, PNG) can only be engraved. Delete duplicate lines – the laser would run over them twice and scorch the edge.',
                ] ],
                [ 'h2', 'Fumes and fire: what to keep in mind' ],
                [ 'p', 'Smoke from wood – and especially from plywood and MDF – contains fine particles, volatile organic compounds and glue breakdown products, so you need extraction to the outside or an air purifier with HEPA and activated carbon filters. Wood can also catch fire, particularly with slow cuts – never leave the laser unattended and keep an extinguisher within reach. We cover laser classes, goggles, filters and fire suppression in our article on safe work with a laser.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which xTool for which wood project?' ],
                [ 'h3', 'First projects and on-site engraving: xTool F2' ],
                [ 'p', 'The portable F2 suits first projects and personalisation at fairs. It has a 15 W diode laser, which according to xTool engraves and cuts wood, acrylic and leather, plus a 5 W IR laser for metals and plastics – useful if you personalise metal as well as wood. The 50 MP camera with live preview makes it easy to place lettering on a finished board or box, and engraving runs at up to 6,000 mm/s. The Slide Extension enlarges the working area for longer boards and signs and comes with 5 clamps. Thicker boards are better cut on the S1 or P3.' ],
                [ 'products', '9163846877397,8041231384789' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Cutting plywood and small batches at home: xTool S1' ],
                [ 'p', 'The S1 is an enclosed Class 1 diode laser with a 20 or 40 W module and a 608 × 385 mm bed that fits several parts at once. Pin-point™ positioning helps you place a design on a finished item, AutoPassthrough™ handles material longer than the bed and 3D Curve™ engraves curved surfaces. Five-direction flame detection and a laser key lock make home use easier. For cutting wood, add the Smart Air Assist and the honeycomb panel.' ],
                [ 'products', '8169201631445,8168720826581,8168720728277' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Colourful decorations and gifts: xTool M2' ],
                [ 'p', 'The M2 has a 10, 20 or 40 W diode laser and a CMYK inkjet module – included in the print bundles or sold separately – that, according to xTool, prints in full colour on wood too. You can print a colourful design on plywood and cut it out straight away – for tags, magnets, Christmas ornaments or puzzles. Two cameras with live preview help you position the design, and the enclosure is Class 1. It is a craft and gift machine; for large boards and batches, the S1 or P3 is the better fit.' ],
